26, 5.232, 9.134, 1.212, 0.6, 12.098, 6.5, 2.856, 5.642, 0.505, 15.745, 9.124, 4.587, 6.93, 3.26, 4.347, 1.7, 10.359, 0.521, 14.868, 2.802, 4.353, 5.127, 11.822, 3.215, 3.297, 13.027, 2.441, 6.872, 5.319, 1.55, 12.242, 10.742, 4.386, 7.668, 2.92, 0.963, 11.493, 15.494, 1.054, 0.078, 3.225, 29.033, 4.573, 3.948, 3.554, 0.065, 24.904, 5.608, 1.636, 0.0, 22.799, 17.356, 2.876, 14.599, 2.539, 11.855, 0.0, 0.0, 14.081, 1.896, 0.0, 0.24, 18.008, 18.546, 3.591], "tempo_bpm": 117.1875, "duration": 245.690375}, "transcript": {"text": "Mother, I need cloth for my new school uniform. Please give us 2 meters of this blue cloth. Certainly, I'll measure it accurately. I'll use this metal measuring rod. Different jobs need different measuring tools. Stand still, I'll take your measurements. Please make the uniform 4 fingers wider in length. Charangula. What does Charangula mean? Charangula means 4 finger widths. An ancient way of measuring. Using your hand span. I got 6 hand spans. Mine is only 5. I counted 7. Because everyone's hands are different, the measurements are different too. Hand span, foot and finger widths vary from person to person. That's why our answers are different. Exactly. Standard units so everyone gets the same measurement. Standard units make measurements fair. India has used measurement systems for thousands of years. Ancient units included Angula, Dhanusa and Yojana. Even today, some traditional carpenters and tailors still use Angula. People once used many different measurement systems. To avoid confusion, countries adopted the international system of units called SI. The SI unit of length is the meter. 1 meter equals 100 centimeters. Each centimeter has 10 millimeters. Millimeter is the smallest division on an ordinary ruler. Large distances use kilometers. Everyday objects use meters. Small objects use centimeters. Very tiny thicknesses use millimeters. Always choose the most suitable unit. Remember these important conversions. 1 kilometer equals 1000 meters. 1 meter equals 100 centimeters. 1 centimeter equals 10 millimeters. Some rulers also show inches. 1 inch equals 2.54 centimeters. Although SI units are standard today, inches and feet are still used by many people. Always place the scale along the object's length. Proper placement gives accurate measurements. Keep your eye directly above the reading. This avoids parallax error. Even a broken scale can be used. Start from another clear mark. Subtract the starting reading from the ending reading. Here, 10.4, 9.4 centimeters. Students with visual impairment use rulers with raised markings. Science should be accessible to everyone. Always leave a space between the number and unit. And write symbols like kilometers, m, c meters and mm in lowercase. Curved lines can be measured using thread. Now we can measure any curve. The school is closer. No, the garden is closer. Everyone is correct because each is measuring from a different place. When everyone measures from the same point, the answers become the same. That fixed place is called the reference point. Let's choose our reference point first. Now we can measure every line correctly. Look, we're getting closer to Delhi. Delhi is the reference point. The passengers look still. Inside the bus, they are at rest. Compared to the road outside, they are moving. Motion in a straight line is called linear motion. Notice the applied force leads to a straight path. Motion along a circular path is called circular motion. To and fro motion about a fixed position is oscillatory motion. When motion repeats after equal intervals of time, it is called periodic motion. Circular and oscillatory motions are examples of periodic motion. Today we learned how to measure accurately, choose the correct units, describe position using a reference point. Thank you for watching. Please subscribe, like and share. Keep learning. See you in the next video.", "language": "english", "quality": "ok", "words": [{"w": "Mother", "s": 0.0, "e": 0.56}, 